ISSUES WITH REDIRECTING TO THE OLD COMMITS:-
# Incase if there is a bug in the code and you move to an older commit. You'll be able to move to the old commit but the code and the contents written after that will be lost.
# You'll be able to redirect to the older commit by using the following command:-
 git checkout space whichisalongnumber
# But while doing this process the rest of the data added will be lost and you'll not be able to recover them. To overcome this issue, branching is done.

BRANCHING:-
# Branch:-For example in a project, each and every projects will be saved in the particluar branch of that page ie; the commit for index.html will be saved on the index commit and for the other about.html it will be saved in a different commit.
# There are the terms to be known:
 ~ Branch: Usually a branch contains the commits for a specific feature or a bugfix.
 ~ Merging: Once a feature or bufix has been developed, that branch gets merged into the main branch.
 ~ Main-branch: Contains the bug free code which is ready to roll out.
 ~ Merging:  Once the feature/ bugfix has been completed the commit should be merged to the main branch.

# We'll be following the below steps for creating the branches:-
 ~ Initialize a repo: git init
 ~ Add files: git add -A
 ~ Commit and add message: git commit -m "Added file index.html"
 ~ Create a branch for the index.html page as follows: git branch feature/index-page
  You'll be able to provide any names for the branches as your wish also make sure to mention if this is a feature or a bugfix.

# Inorder to switch to another branch we can make use of the following command:-
  git switch branchname
# To display all the branches we should use the following command by using the graph keyword:-
 git log --all --graph

# The bugfree commit should be merged to the main/master branch once the commits are done. To do this, we should do as follows:-
 ~ At first move back to the master commit.
 ~ Then run the following command: git merge feature/about-page

CODE REVIEW:-
# When you're working in a company, your code is being reviewed by a software engineer before it gets merged to main/master branch. This process is called a code review.
# Once the commit and the commit and updating is being finished we'll have to add the files from the local system to remote repo in Github. To do this do as follows:
 ~ Create a repo by providing a name and copy the command of:
     git remote add origin https://github.com/irfanailyas/entri-code-review-repository.git
 Here origin is the name of the remote.
 ~ Then we should be adding each branches to the repo as follows:
    Move to the master branch at first by using the command git switch master
    Then push the branch as 
    git push origin master
    git push origin feature/index-page
    git push origin feature/about-page
